# 🤖 AI-Powered Task & Action Item Extractor

An automated workflow built with Python and Google Gemini API that transforms unstructured meeting notes, emails, and raw transcripts into structured action items, task matrices, and risk assessments.

[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/TU_USUARIO/TU_REPOSITORIO/blob/main/task_extractor_gemini.ipynb)

## 📌 Problem Statement
In fast-paced project teams, professionals spend critical hours manually reviewing meeting transcripts and converting unstructured conversations into organized action items. This manual process often leads to missed deadlines, untracked dependencies, and operational overhead.

## 💡 Solution
This project automates task extraction using LLMs (`gemini-3.8-flash`). By applying structured prompt constraints and low-temperature sampling, the script parses raw text and outputs:
1. Executive Summary
2. Structured Task Matrix (Task, Assignee, Priority, Deadline)
3. Identified Risks & Bottlenecks
4. Immediate Next Steps

## 🛠️ Tech Stack
- **Language:** Python 3.10+
- **AI Model:** Google Gemini API (`google-genai` SDK)
- **Environment:** Google Colab / Jupyter Notebooks

## 🚀 How to Run

### Option 1: Run in Google Colab
1. Click the **Open in Colab** badge above.
2. Get a free Gemini API Key from [Google AI Studio](https://aistudio.google.com/).
3. Set your key in Colab Secrets (`GEMINI_API_KEY`) or in the code variable.
4. Run all cells.

## 📊 Output Example

Input Raw Text:
> "Carlos needs to fix the server latencies by Friday. Maria mentioned the client asked about the UI release..."

Generated Output:
| Task | Assignee | Priority | Suggested Deadline |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Server Index Optimization | Carlos | High | Friday |
| Send Testing Credentials | Juan | High | Today |
| UI Prototype Approval | Juan | Medium | Thursday |

## 📈 Key Learnings & Impact
- Reduced manual task extraction time from ~15 minutes to under 5 seconds.
- Enforced reliable Markdown table output using zero-shot system prompts.
- Maintained output consistency by tuning model temperature settings (`temperature=0.2`).
